    },
    {
      "id": "c18-l2",
      "title": "First In, First Out",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A queue is a waiting line: new things join at the back, and leave from the front. The first one in is the first one out.",
        "With an array, keep two positions: head (where to take from) and tail (where to add). To reuse the space, both wrap around with % SIZE: a ring buffer.",
        "Unix used queues everywhere: characters typed on a terminal waited in one, and so did files sent to the line printer."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "#define SIZE 4",
          "int q[SIZE];",
          "int head = 0, tail = 0, count = 0;",
          "",
          "void put(int x) {",
          "    q[tail] = x;",
          "    tail = (tail + 1) % SIZE;",
          "    count++;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int get() {",
          "    int x = q[head];",
          "    head = (head + 1) % SIZE;",
          "    count--;",
          "    return x;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    put(1);",
          "    put(2);",
          "    put(3);",
          "    printf(\"%d\\n\", get());",
          "    put(4);",
          "    put(5);",
          "    while (count > 0)",
          "        printf(\"%d \", get());",
          "    printf(\"\\n\");",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "1",
          "2 3 4 5 "
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c18-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What comes out of a queue first?",
          "options": [
            "The first thing put in",
            "The last thing put in",
            "The biggest",
            "The newest"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "First in, first out: FIFO."
        },
        {
          "id": "c18-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why does the example use % SIZE?",
          "options": [
            "So the positions wrap around and reuse the array",
            "To make it faster",
            "To sort the queue",
            "To count the elements"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "After the last slot, the next one is slot 0 again."
        },
        {
          "id": "c18-l2-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Move tail forward, wrapping around.",
          "code": [
            "    tail = (tail + 1) ___ SIZE;"
          ],
          "options": [
            "%",
            "/",
            "*",
            "-"
          ],
          "answer": "%",
          "explanation": "The remainder keeps it between 0 and SIZE - 1."
        },
        {
          "id": "c18-l2-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "",
            "int q[8];",
            "int head = 0, tail = 0;",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    q[tail++] = 5;",
            "    q[tail++] = 6;",
            "    q[tail++] = 7;",
            "    int a = q[head++];",
            "    int b = q[head++];",
            "    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", a, b);",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "options": [
            "5 6",
            "6 5",
            "5 7",
            "7 6"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Queues keep the order: 5 went in first."
        },
        {
          "id": "c18-l2-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Which of these is a queue?",
          "options": [
            "Jobs waiting for the printer",
            "The undo history",
            "A pile of plates",
            "Nested brackets"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The job sent first gets printed first."
        },
        {
          "id": "c18-l2-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does the count variable protect against?",
          "options": [
            "Getting from an empty queue, or putting into a full one",
            "Wrong order",
            "Slow programs",
            "Nothing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Check count > 0 before get, and count < SIZE before put."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c18-l3",
      "title": "Built from Lists",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "An array stack has a fixed size. A linked list stack can grow as long as there's memory: push adds a node at the front, pop removes the front node and frees it.",
        "struct node *top; is the whole stack. Empty means top == NULL.",
        "This is the kind of structure inside a compiler: it uses a stack to match brackets and to work out 2 + 3 * 4."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "#include <stdlib.h>",
          "",
          "struct node {",
          "    int value;",
          "    struct node *next;",
          "};",
          "",
          "struct node *top = NULL;",
          "",
          "void push(int v) {",
          "    struct node *n = malloc(sizeof(struct node));",
          "    n->value = v;",
          "    n->next = top;",
          "    top = n;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int pop() {",
          "    struct node *n = top;",
          "    int v = n->value;",
          "    top = n->next;",
          "    free(n);",
          "    return v;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int i;",
          "    for (i = 1; i <= 5; i++)",
          "        push(i * 100);",
          "    while (top != NULL)",
          "        printf(\"%d \", pop());",
          "    printf(\"\\n\");",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "500 400 300 200 100 "
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c18-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "When is a list stack empty?",
          "options": [
            "When top is NULL",
            "When top->value is 0",
            "After 100 pushes",
            "Never"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "No nodes left means no top."
        },
        {
          "id": "c18-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why does pop save the value before calling free?",
          "options": [
            "After free, the node can't be read anymore",
            "free changes the value",
            "To make it faster",
            "It doesn't need to"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Read first, free after."
        },
        {
          "id": "c18-l3-e3",
          "type": "order_lines",
          "prompt": "Put the lines of pop in the right order.",
          "lines": [
            "struct node *n = top;",
            "int v = n->value;",
            "top = n->next;",
            "free(n);",
            "return v;"
          ],
          "answer": [
            "struct node *n = top;",
            "int v = n->value;",
            "top = n->next;",
            "free(n);",
            "return v;"
          ],
          "explanation": "Remember the node, take its value, move top, free, return."
        },
        {
          "id": "c18-l3-e4",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "A new node goes on top of the old top.",
          "code": [
            "    n->next = ___;",
            "    top = n;"
          ],
          "options": [
            "top",
            "NULL",
            "n",
            "next"
          ],
          "answer": "top",
          "explanation": "The new node points to the old top."
        },
        {
          "id": "c18-l3-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is the advantage over the array stack?",
          "options": [
            "It has no fixed size limit",
            "It's simpler",
            "It doesn't use pointers",
            "It doesn't need free"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "It grows one node at a time."
        },
        {
          "id": "c18-l3-e6",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This pop crashes on an empty stack. Which line needs a check before it?",
          "code": [
            "int pop() {",
            "    struct node *n = top;",
            "    int v = n->value;",
            "    top = n->next;",
            "    free(n);",
            "    return v;",
            "}"
          ],
          "answer": 2,
          "explanation": "If top is NULL, n->value crashes. Check if (top == NULL) first."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
